PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains a unanimous consensus in favor of Choice D (Atrial septal defect). The core finding anchoring this diagnosis is the specific electrocardiographic combination of left axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy, which is classic for an ostium primum atrial septal defect. The systolic ejection murmur, radiation toward the clavicle, and palpable thrill reflect a functional pulmonic flow murmur generated by high-volume left-to-right shunting rather than isolated organic pulmonic valve stenosis. Furthermore, the patient's adult presentation with exertional dyspnea alongside a remote history of transient childhood cyanosis aligns directly with the natural history of an unrepaired interatrial communication.
}

11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains a complete, unanimous consensus on Choice D (Atrial septal defect). The clinical presentation of an adult presenting with exertional dyspnea, a functional pulmonic flow murmur with thrill, and transient childhood cyanosis aligns directly with an uncorrected ostium primum atrial septal defect. Most decisively, the combination of left axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram is the classic hallmark of an ostium primum defect rather than isolated pulmonic stenosis, confirming Choice D as the correct diagnosis.
}

